#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059533 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/id #uuid "a3f9e797-4bfb-5b2a-b460-98d7def3ae20" :question/math-academy-id "e-6754" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arccos\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).$" :question/worked-solution "The range of $\\theta = \\arccos x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0 \\le \\theta  \\le \\pi.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left( \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arccos\\left(  -\\dfrac{1}{2} \\right) =\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059534 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3878b5fa-fd5e-5984-8516-96ee17bf137b" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arctangent"}] :question/id #uuid "83fc553b-3ad0-5003-afe3-81204a695162" :question/math-academy-id "e-6755" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the value of $\\arctan(-\\sqrt 3).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arctan x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\lt \\theta \\lt \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta$ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\sqrt 3$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the fourth quadrant, where the tangent is negative.\n\nFrom the special values of the trigonometric functions and the oddness of the tangent function, we know that \n\n$$\n\\tan \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\sqrt 3.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ is an angle in the fourth quadrant that lies within the range of $\\arctan,$ we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\arctan \\left(-\\sqrt 3\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059544 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/id #uuid "a2641aac-7c3a-5a2c-97f0-aca4782c7215" :question/math-academy-id "e-7220" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}} 2 \\right).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta = \\arcsin x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\dfrac\\pi 2 \\le \\theta \\le \\dfrac\\pi 2.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\sin\\left( \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\\right) = -\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta.$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$ and lies within the range of $\\theta,$ we subtract $2\\pi{:}$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} - 2\\pi = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\arcsin \\left(-\\dfrac {\\sqrt{3}} 2\\right) = -\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34cf8c59-80ca-5c6c-b50e-e796548538f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "0c25fa5d-ccc5-55e4-b3e1-06492f830655" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295363} {:answer/id #uuid "074bbb58-87f2-52d5-992f-e8e7e621ed0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{150}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295364} {:answer/id #uuid "0bd5ab0e-5ef6-5a77-9dbe-a0573330be0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295365} {:answer/id #uuid "07372dce-4325-5ece-bc95-8233bc42e4b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295366}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34cf8c59-80ca-5c6c-b50e-e796548538f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} :answer-field/id #uuid "89d8dedf-331e-5615-8a14-a67f86043945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1796fbf1-69e9-550e-8c0e-6de576d0b219" :question/math-academy-id "q-117408" :question/problem "$2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =2\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-{90}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba1de734-7e89-5775-be96-708770a71621" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295383} {:answer/id #uuid "ae92e186-89d2-5d1b-80fb-ad5312c685a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "d892dc8f-e331-55db-8bee-de86e7e620b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{180}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295385} {:answer/id #uuid "1a3a434a-a6c5-5eb7-ae9c-86d22b2f38c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} {:answer/id #uuid "1421e17c-d81d-5463-8845-a52eade9f26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295387}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1421e17c-d81d-5463-8845-a52eade9f26c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5132a217-dfb9-5445-a0bd-3fe3bc49b29f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295382}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8183a61b-58d8-541a-bebb-58f44b6bf474" :question/math-academy-id "q-24756" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and fourth quadrants.\n\nSince $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive, our answer is an angle that lies in the first quadrant, where sine is positive.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14035417/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values in the first quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})={30}^{∘}.\n$$"}]}
